From 309f27ac27c058b81228bc26d71202b4f884c278 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 22:54:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the read retry only for a player, stopped
 through Blu-ray and DVD images too

Two gaps of the two commits before, found by a review:

- A Blu-ray or a DVD played from an image on a local drive reads it through a CDVDInputStreamFile
  inside CDVDInputStreamBluray or CDVDInputStreamNavigator. The player's Abort() reaches only the
  outer stream (CDVDInputStreamBluray::Abort() set HOLD_EXIT, the navigator had none), so a stop
  during a retry waited for the whole window, inside bd_read() with libbluray's mutex held. Now
  the outer stream keeps the flag, its Abort() sets it, and the inner stream's tries read it
  (CDVDInputStreamFile::RetryFailedReads(abort)): the outer stream never touches m_pstream from the
  thread that closes, as Close() may reset it meanwhile.
- The demuxer gave the wait of a read back to its deadline only for DVDSTREAM_TYPE_FILE: a Blu-ray
  or DVD read from a local image or folder still ended as in RC6. Now
  CDemuxReadStall::LocalInput() takes a file or a disc (Blu-ray, DVD) on a local path.
- The retry was on for every CDVDInputStreamFile, also for readers that never call Abort()
  (CDVDFileInfo for thumbnails and stream details, subtitle files, vobsub): a bad sector could
  hold a thumbnail for 15 s. Now it is asked for where a player reads: CDVDFactoryInputStream
  when it is given a player (CVideoPlayer and its multi-source input), and the Blu-ray and DVD
  streams when they have one. Everything else reads as in RC6.

Host test: demuxstall_test.cpp has the rule of the inputs (a disc on a local path gives the wait
back; network paths and other inputs do not); with the rule of the first version it fails.
readretry_test.cpp passes unchanged. The abort through the image streams and the choice of the
readers are read and compiled (syntax check with the build's own commands), not run: there is
no host test of the Blu-ray or DVD streams.
